5.3  How a picture reaches the screen: KMS, Wayland and X11

TL;DR
  - The kernel sets up the screen itself (KMS: kernel mode setting): resolution,
    refresh rate, which output shows which image.
  - Each window draws into its own buffer. A compositor combines them into one
    image and asks the kernel to show it at the next refresh.
  - On Wayland the compositor is the display server (GNOME, KDE, Sway). On X11,
    the X server sits in between. Old X11 apps run on Wayland through XWayland.

      echo $XDG_SESSION_TYPE                 # wayland or x11
      cat /sys/class/drm/*/status            # which outputs have a screen

On your machine
  card0     Intel, driver i915, shows the boot screen
  Screens   eDP-1 1920x1200 · free: DP-1, HDMI-A-1
  Compute   /dev/dri/renderD128

Pitfalls
  - nomodeset on the kernel command line disables KMS: a basic framebuffer with
    no acceleration. Useful to get a broken system to a desktop, never to keep
    (20.2).
  - Screen sharing and global hotkeys work differently on Wayland: apps go
    through portals (PipeWire), not by reading the whole screen.
  - Tearing is an image changed in the middle of a refresh. Compositors avoid it
    by flipping only between refreshes, at a small latency cost.

Deep dive

  ▾ What happens 60 times a second?

    When a window has a new frame, it hands the compositor a buffer, often
    already in GPU memory. The compositor draws all visible windows into a new
    screen buffer with the GPU, then asks the kernel to flip: show this buffer
    from the next vertical blank, the pause between two refreshes. The kernel
    tells it when the flip happened, and the cycle starts again.

  ▾ Why did Wayland replace X11?

    X11's design dates from 1984: the server drew everything itself, over the
    network if needed. Modern desktops bypassed most of it, leaving a complex
    middleman with weak isolation: any X11 app can read the keyboard and the
    screen of every other. Wayland makes the compositor the server, and each app
    sees only its own windows.

Related
  Commands  echo, cat
  Paths     /sys/class/drm/
